        exp: "Rule: Under modern premises liability and Restatement (Second) of Torts § 343A, while the traditional 'natural accumulation' doctrine historically protected landowners from liability for snow and ice, modern law holds that a commercial possessor or common carrier operating facilities open to the public owes invitees an affirmative duty of reasonable care to maintain primary entry and exit routes in a reasonably safe condition, even against open and obvious winter weather conditions, where the possessor should anticipate that invitees will encounter the hazard out of practical necessity to access the facility. Furthermore, failing to adhere to its own established safety protocol (salting every thirty minutes) while a janitor sat idle demonstrates a triable issue of breach of reasonable care, defeating summary judgment (Option C). Option A is incorrect because modern tort law rejects blanket immunity for natural accumulations on primary commercial entryways, especially for common carriers. Option B is incorrect because walking down a primary subway staircase to catch a train is not voluntary assumption of the risk, but rather raises a comparative fault issue for the jury. Option D is incorrect because transit authorities are held to negligence standards, not strict liability."
