        exp: "Rule: At common law, burglary requires: (1) breaking, (2) entering, (3) the dwelling house, (4) of another, (5) in the nighttime, (6) with the intent to commit a felony therein. A **breaking** requires the application of physical force to create an opening (such as prying open a locked latch). An **entry** requires that some part of the defendant's body (a hand, foot, or finger) or an instrument used to consummate the ultimate felony (such as a hook to snatch jewelry) physically pass across the interior threshold of the building. Using a pry bar solely to effectuate a breaking does not satisfy the entry requirement. Because Defendant shattered the latch (breaking) with felonious intent at night, but was interrupted before any entry occurred, the burglary was not completed. However, intending to commit burglary and taking a substantial, overt step directly toward its execution constitutes completed **attempted burglary** (Option B). Option A is incorrect because entry is an indispensable element of completed burglary that was not satisfied. Option C is incorrect because attempted burglary is an aggravated felony that ranks above attempted larceny. Option D is incorrect because Defendant progressed far beyond mere trespass into attempted burglary."
