        ans: 2,
        exp: "Rule: Under the common law of contracts (Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 84), a party who is the beneficiary of an express condition precedent (such as a time-of-the-essence delivery date) may waive that condition by words or conduct. While an executory waiver made without consideration can ordinarily be retracted before the time for the condition has expired, a waiver CANNOT be retracted if the other party has materially and detrimentally changed his position in reliance on the waiver (e.g., opting not to pay a $5,000 rush fee and pacing work to deliver on the extended date). The developer waived the August 1 deadline, and the engineering firm foreseeably and detrimentally relied on that waiver, rendering the waiver irrevocable. The firm is entitled to the full $40,000 contract price (Option C). Option A is incorrect because an express condition can be waived orally and made irrevocable through detrimental reliance without satisfying the Statute of Frauds. Option B is incorrect because the parol evidence rule applies only to agreements prior to or contemporaneous with signing, not subsequent waivers. Option D is incorrect because substantial performance cannot override an express condition; the firm prevails on *waiver*."
