        fp: "A manufacturer of consumer electronics designed an electric space heater equipped with a mechanical tip-over cutoff switch designed to cut power if the heater tipped past 45 degrees. The heater was distributed to a retail department store, which sold it to a consumer. The consumer placed the space heater on a bedside nightstand. One evening, the consumer's pet cat knocked the space heater off the nightstand onto a shag carpet. Due to a manufacturing defect in the internal tip-over switch, the electrical contacts fused together, preventing the power cutoff from engaging. The ceramic heating element remained energized at full heat against the carpet fibers, igniting a bedroom fire. The consumer was asleep in the adjacent room. Woken by smoke alarms, the consumer ran into the bedroom and saw the burning heater. Knowing that his infant daughter's bedroom was down the hallway, the consumer picked up the burning space heater with his bare hands to throw it out a window. The consumer suffered severe third-degree burns across both hands and forearms. The consumer brought a strict products liability action against the space heater manufacturer.",
