        fp: "A homeowner contracted in writing with a licensed residential roofing contractor to install a new architectural shingle roof on his house for $16,000, payable upon completion on September 1. The contract contained no express warranties, but did not disclaim any warranties. On August 28, the contractor completed the roof installation, and the homeowner paid the full $16,000 contract price. Two weeks later, during a normal, moderate rainstorm, water leaked through the roof in four separate locations, causing $5,000 in water damage to ceilings, drywall, and hardwood flooring. A certified roofing inspector examined the roof and discovered that the contractor's crew had improperly spaced the shingle overlaps and had failed to install rubberized leak-barrier membrane in the roof valleys, in direct violation of basic trade standards and manufacturer installation specifications. The homeowner sued the contractor for breach of contract, seeking $5,000 for the damaged drywall and flooring, plus $6,000 to repair and re-flash the roof.",
