        exp: "Rule: Under the landmark rule of *Hadley v. Baxendale* and Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 351, an injured party is entitled to recover consequential damages resulting from a breach of contract if the damages were reasonably foreseeable to the breaching party at the time the contract was formed. Damages are foreseeable if they follow from the breach in the ordinary course of events as a natural and probable result. When a roofing contractor defectively installs a residential roof, water penetration into the home during a normal rainstorm causing damage to interior ceilings, drywall, and hardwood floors is the classic, natural, and highly foreseeable consequence of a leaky roof. The homeowner is fully entitled to recover the $5,000 in consequential property damages in addition to direct repair costs (Option C). Option A is incorrect because consequential property damage naturally flowing from defective construction is fully compensable. Option B is incorrect because rain is a normal natural occurrence in the ordinary course of events that requires no special advance warning. Option D is incorrect because a contract to install a residential roof is predominantly a service contract governed by common law, not a sale of goods under UCC Article 2."
